WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.000
You know, usually when we talk about a prison, we picture walls.

00:04.420 --> 00:05.500
Right. Concrete steel.

00:05.700 --> 00:11.360
Yeah, exactly. We picture the concrete, the heavy steel bars, the guard towers, the coiled barbed wire.

00:11.860 --> 00:16.360
It is a structure built with, you know, immense deliberate intentionality.

00:16.760 --> 00:18.100
Someone had to actually design it.

00:18.120 --> 00:23.360
Right. Someone, somewhere, sat down at a drafting table, drew up a blueprint,

00:23.900 --> 00:27.820
and calculated exactly how high those walls needed to be to keep you inside.

00:27.820 --> 00:31.280
The intent is just obvious from the moment the first trench is dug.

00:31.800 --> 00:38.880
I mean, you look at it and you immediately understand that the entire purpose of the architecture is enclosure and control.

00:39.540 --> 00:43.880
It's physical, it's overt, and it's unapologetic.

00:44.040 --> 00:47.560
But what if the most inescapable prison wasn't built with concrete at all?

00:47.740 --> 00:50.100
What if it was built with something completely invisible?

00:50.360 --> 00:53.820
What if it was built out of the vocabulary you use every single day?

00:53.920 --> 00:54.180
Right.

00:54.180 --> 00:57.020
And what if, and this is the part that genuinely blew my mind,

00:57.020 --> 00:59.280
nobody even planned to build it in the first place?

00:59.840 --> 01:07.580
It completely upends our standard, sort of cinematic understanding of how corporate power and monopolies are actually formed.

01:07.680 --> 01:17.320
We have this tendency to assume that world domination requires, like, a boardroom full of evil masterminds drawing up sinister 10-year plans.

01:17.340 --> 01:18.820
Right. The supervillain table.

01:18.980 --> 01:19.420
Exactly.

01:19.420 --> 01:24.700
But the reality this research uncovers suggests that you don't always need a master plan.

01:25.460 --> 01:31.880
Sometimes you just need a few minor, seemingly insignificant historical accidents that nobody ever bothers to correct.

01:32.660 --> 01:33.900
Welcome to the Deep Dive.

01:34.000 --> 01:35.760
Today we have a really specific mission.

01:36.180 --> 01:41.900
We are taking a shortcut to being deeply well-informed by extracting the core insights from a single,

01:42.440 --> 01:46.480
truly fascinating piece of source material that you absolutely need to know about.

01:46.480 --> 01:47.180
That's a great one.

01:47.280 --> 01:52.000
We are digging into a massive 2026 monograph by the independent researcher Flyxion.

01:52.520 --> 01:58.480
It is titled Vocabulary as Moat, Microsoft DOS, and the Politics of Proprietary Convention.

01:58.800 --> 02:02.220
And it is honestly a monumental piece of technological archaeology.

02:02.300 --> 02:03.040
It really is.

02:03.180 --> 02:07.500
What Flyxion does is take something that most of us experience as purely functional, almost invisible.

02:08.160 --> 02:12.960
I mean, the names of the computer commands we type, the specific direction of a slash on a keyboard,

02:12.960 --> 02:15.040
or the phrasing of an error message on a screen.

02:15.200 --> 02:16.440
Stuff you don't even think about.

02:16.620 --> 02:17.060
Exactly.

02:17.540 --> 02:21.740
And reads them as a highly detailed historical map of corporate lock-in.

02:21.880 --> 02:22.960
Okay, let's unpack this.

02:23.000 --> 02:29.340
Because you, listening right now, you turn on a computer or a phone, and the interface just feels natural, right?

02:29.580 --> 02:29.780
Yeah.

02:29.860 --> 02:32.580
You tap an icon, you swipe a screen, you type a web address.

02:32.660 --> 02:33.620
It feels like gravity.

02:34.000 --> 02:34.600
It just is.

02:34.600 --> 02:45.680
But this deep dive reveals how the words and symbols on your screens are actually ancient, unyielding walls designed to trap you in an ecosystem.

02:46.340 --> 02:57.280
We are going to explore how something as mundane as using a backslash instead of a forward slash quietly built one of the most powerful commercial fortresses in human history.

02:57.520 --> 03:03.700
To understand how these invisible walls were constructed, we actually have to ignore Microsoft for a moment and look at the foundation they built upon.

03:03.700 --> 03:04.660
Right, we have to go back.

03:04.740 --> 03:05.900
That takes us back to 1980.

03:06.200 --> 03:12.820
The origin story of MS-DOS is absolutely not a story about a brilliant startup trying to conquer the globe.

03:12.960 --> 03:13.600
Not at all.

03:13.840 --> 03:20.700
It is a story about a very stressed out programmer dealing with a very specific annoying hardware constraint.

03:21.580 --> 03:23.060
His name was Tim Patterson.

03:23.200 --> 03:27.840
He worked at Seattle Computer Products, and he was writing an operating system called 86 Deals.

03:28.540 --> 03:32.060
And crucially, his goal was not to reinvent the wheel here.

03:32.060 --> 03:42.700
He wasn't trying to build some philosophical alternative to Unix, which was, you know, the incredibly powerful, elegant operating system being developed over Bell Labs at the time.

03:42.820 --> 03:43.360
Not at all.

03:43.900 --> 03:46.060
Unix was born from a pure research culture.

03:46.880 --> 03:50.060
The engineers at Bell Labs sat around debating grand design philosophies.

03:50.700 --> 03:51.940
They had the time and the budget.

03:52.060 --> 03:52.520
Exactly.

03:53.020 --> 03:56.100
And the physical environment they worked in dictated their vocabulary.

03:56.100 --> 04:03.140
They were typing on teletypes, essentially massive, loud, mechanical typewriters hooked up to a mainframe.

04:03.260 --> 04:03.880
Oh, wow.

04:04.000 --> 04:08.360
Yeah, they printed on physical paper, and they were incredibly slow, maybe 10 characters a second.

04:08.460 --> 04:09.800
That sounds agonizing.

04:10.260 --> 04:16.700
Because of that physical constraint, Unix developers valued terribly terse composable tools.

04:16.700 --> 04:22.860
That is why Unix commands are things like key for copy, MV for move, or Ls for list.

04:23.360 --> 04:26.320
Every keystroke was a physical effort and a waste of time.

04:26.720 --> 04:26.780
Right.

04:26.920 --> 04:30.720
But Tim Patterson at Seattle Computer Products was in a completely different universe.

04:30.940 --> 04:31.980
He wasn't in a research lab.

04:32.020 --> 04:33.000
He was trying to sell hardware.

04:33.260 --> 04:33.360
Yep.

04:33.800 --> 04:45.320
He just needed a functional operating system for the new Intel 8086 computer boards because the industry standard software at the time, which was digital research as CPM, was severely delayed.

04:45.420 --> 04:46.080
It was late, yeah.

04:46.080 --> 04:53.260
So we had a pragmatic commercial mandate, clone the interface of CPM so that developers could easily port their existing software over.

04:53.580 --> 04:58.160
That concept, source-level portability, is the engine of this entire story.

04:58.340 --> 05:11.540
If you were a software developer in 1980 and you had spent a year writing code that worked perfectly on CPM, Patterson wanted you to be able to bring that software over to his new system, 86thedewis, without rewriting it from scratch.

05:11.540 --> 05:11.720
Yeah.

05:11.720 --> 05:22.740
So 86th DOS inherited CPM's conventions wholesale, not because they were philosophically superior to Unix, but purely because they were already familiar to the people with the money.

05:22.740 --> 05:25.280
And this is exactly where Microsoft enters the picture.

05:25.780 --> 05:33.220
They needed an operating system to supply to IBM for the highly secretive, highly rushed IBM PC project.

05:33.480 --> 05:34.600
The chess pieces are moving.

05:34.720 --> 05:34.880
Yeah.

05:35.120 --> 05:40.260
They found Patterson's 86th DOS, licensed it, and then fully bought it in 1981.

05:40.720 --> 05:44.140
It became PC DOS for IBM and MS DOS for everyone else.

05:44.140 --> 05:52.400
Microsoft didn't design the core interface, they bought a clone of CPM because the familiarity Patterson built into it was a massive selling point.

05:52.400 --> 05:58.220
Which leads us directly to one of the most famous, and frankly most annoying, keyboard characters in history.

05:58.340 --> 05:58.880
The slash.

05:59.060 --> 05:59.600
The slash.

05:59.740 --> 06:03.460
If you were listening to this and you've ever typed a web address, you know you use a forward slash.

06:03.840 --> 06:09.140
But if you have ever poked around the inner file workings of a Windows computer, you have seen the backslash.

06:09.280 --> 06:10.400
Yeah, leaning the wrong way.

06:10.660 --> 06:10.900
Right.

06:11.320 --> 06:12.120
Why are they different?

06:12.400 --> 06:15.100
Why does Windows lean backwards when the rest of the world leans forwards?

06:15.700 --> 06:18.540
Flyxion's research traces the genealogy perfectly.

06:18.940 --> 06:21.560
And it starts with a completely different piece of machinery.

06:21.900 --> 06:30.880
In CPM and in the old DEC mini-computer manuals that CPM was heavily copying, the forward slash was used as a switch.

06:31.100 --> 06:32.280
Yes, a command switch.

06:32.540 --> 06:33.400
Think of it as a modifier.

06:33.980 --> 06:38.120
If you typed a command to read a file, but you only wanted to read it one page at a time,

06:38.120 --> 06:42.520
you might type a forward slash followed by a P for pause.

06:43.280 --> 06:47.780
The slash told the computer, hey, what comes next is an option, not a file name.

06:48.000 --> 06:51.900
Like flipping a physical toggle switch on a piece of hardware to change its behavior?

06:52.120 --> 06:52.960
Exactly that metaphor.

06:53.180 --> 06:53.400
Yeah.

06:53.760 --> 06:57.300
Meanwhile, over the Unix world, they were evolving completely separately.

06:57.500 --> 07:00.460
They hadn't even fully standardized their own command modifiers yet,

07:00.460 --> 07:04.940
but they were slowly settling on the dash or the hyphen for their options.

07:05.720 --> 07:08.080
So you have two distinct evolutionary lineages.

07:08.500 --> 07:14.360
DOS inherited the forward slash as a switch from CPM, and Unix was moving toward the dash.

07:14.520 --> 07:19.120
But in the early 1980s with DOS 1.0, this wasn't a problem at all.

07:19.200 --> 07:19.540
Not yet.

07:19.540 --> 07:22.240
Because DOS 1.0 didn't have folders.

07:22.440 --> 07:24.120
It didn't have hierarchical directories.

07:24.240 --> 07:25.380
You just had a floppy disk.

07:25.580 --> 07:30.880
And when you typed DIR, you saw a flat list of every single file on that disk.

07:30.960 --> 07:31.080
Right.

07:31.160 --> 07:32.080
It was all just right there.

07:32.180 --> 07:36.240
So the forward slash was perfectly happy just acting as a switch.

07:36.540 --> 07:38.720
There was no real estate conflict on the keyboard.

07:38.880 --> 07:40.780
But that all shatters in 1983.

07:41.680 --> 07:44.680
IBM releases the PC XT.

07:44.680 --> 07:50.880
And this machine features a piece of hardware that completely changes the paradigm of personal computing.

07:51.240 --> 07:51.820
The hard drive.

07:51.980 --> 07:53.480
A 10 megabyte hard drive.

07:53.580 --> 07:54.220
10 megabyte.

07:54.280 --> 07:55.840
It sounds like a joke now.

07:56.180 --> 07:58.700
I mean, a single photo on your phone is larger than that.

07:58.900 --> 08:02.160
But at the time, that was unfathomable power.

08:02.540 --> 08:03.840
It was an ocean of storage.

08:04.440 --> 08:06.180
But here is the architectural crisis.

08:06.480 --> 08:09.760
You cannot have a flat list of files when you have 10 megabytes of storage.

08:09.880 --> 08:11.700
You would have thousands of files.

08:11.740 --> 08:12.500
It would be chaos.

08:12.500 --> 08:15.720
Users would scroll forever trying to find a single document.

08:16.240 --> 08:21.100
So for DOS 2.0, Microsoft absolutely had to introduce hierarchical directories.

08:21.220 --> 08:23.960
They needed the concept of putting folders inside of folders.

08:24.780 --> 08:26.980
And Flyxion points out an incredible detail here.

08:27.260 --> 08:33.400
Microsoft borrowed the concept of the hierarchical directory tree directly, without apology, from Unix.

08:33.640 --> 08:36.700
Wait, if they borrowed the architecture from Unix, why didn't they borrow the vocabulary?

08:37.220 --> 08:40.280
Unix used the forward slash to separate folders.

08:40.280 --> 08:41.740
Well, they actually tried to.

08:41.740 --> 08:47.760
Microsoft engineers at the time were simultaneously working on their own licensed version of Unix called Xenix.

08:47.960 --> 08:49.020
Oh, right.

08:49.340 --> 08:52.200
So they were intimately familiar with how Bill Labs did this.

08:52.700 --> 08:59.900
They even adopted the Unix convention of using a single dot to represent the current folder and two dots to represent the parent folder.

08:59.900 --> 09:10.060
And if you look at the surviving development materials for DOS 2.0, the engineers initially used the forward slash to separate these new directories, exactly like Unix.

09:10.060 --> 09:21.420
But if they're using the forward slash for folders and all the legacy CPM software they promised to support expects the forward slash to mean command switch, wouldn't that just hopelessly crash the parser?

09:21.420 --> 09:22.660
That's the exact collision.

09:22.660 --> 09:28.220
How does the computer know if slash P means the folder named P or pause the screen?

09:28.560 --> 09:29.220
It doesn't.

09:29.520 --> 09:31.700
It was a massive system crisis waiting to happen.

09:32.040 --> 09:35.420
The IBM-facing version, BC-DOS, required a settlement.

09:35.940 --> 09:39.180
IBM demanded absolute backwards compatibility.

09:39.520 --> 09:41.020
You could not break the old software.

09:41.020 --> 09:48.260
So, to preserve the forward slash for legacy switch commands, Microsoft just flipped the directory slash.

09:48.500 --> 09:51.520
They settled on the back slash as the path separator.

09:52.000 --> 09:56.000
It was a hasty patch to solve a specific immediate parser conflict.

09:56.180 --> 09:58.720
I love this because it is so incredibly human.

09:58.880 --> 10:01.700
I picture it like moving into a quirky old house.

10:01.800 --> 10:08.720
You move in and you find a weird light switch in the hallway that, for some inexplicable reason, controls the garage door across the street.

10:08.900 --> 10:09.080
Right.

10:09.080 --> 10:13.080
Now, you could tear out the walls, trace the wiring, and fix it properly.

10:13.260 --> 10:17.240
But doing that means rewiring the entire neighborhood and angering all your neighbors.

10:17.300 --> 10:17.760
Too expensive.

10:17.900 --> 10:18.500
Too much hassle.

10:18.620 --> 10:19.200
So you don't.

10:19.260 --> 10:19.980
You just leave it.

10:20.280 --> 10:25.720
But eventually, years later, that weird light switch defines how you navigate the dark in your own home.

10:26.060 --> 10:27.720
You build your daily habits around it.

10:27.920 --> 10:34.720
But how do we go from a quirky, accidental patch on a keyboard to a trillion-dollar competitive moat?

10:34.720 --> 10:39.640
If we connect this to the bigger picture, the origin of the backslash was entirely innocent.

10:40.460 --> 10:48.180
As Flyxion argues brilliantly, nobody at Microsoft in 1981 or 1983 masterminded a plan to lock out Unix users.

10:48.540 --> 10:48.720
Yeah.

10:48.920 --> 10:51.180
The backslash was pure path dependence.

10:51.320 --> 10:52.540
It was a localized compromise.

10:52.540 --> 10:59.140
But the real political object of interest, the actual core of the moat, is what happened after the crisis passed.

10:59.220 --> 11:00.380
When they had the chance to fix it.

11:00.500 --> 11:00.880
Exactly.

11:01.420 --> 11:09.100
Microsoft declined every single subsequent opportunity to reconsider the backslash long after that initial compatibility rationale expired.

11:09.100 --> 11:10.760
That is the pivotal shift in the narrative.

11:11.220 --> 11:14.740
The accident calcifies into a deliberate, unyielding structure.

11:15.040 --> 11:18.680
And that takes us directly into how words literally became walls.

11:19.300 --> 11:21.640
Let's look at the actual vocabulary of enclosure.

11:21.800 --> 11:23.440
To see the walls, look at the command names.

11:23.580 --> 11:27.460
We talked about Unix's terse commands, born from slow, mechanical teletypes.

11:27.700 --> 11:30.140
CP, RM, MVLS, CAT.

11:30.760 --> 11:34.260
DOS, on the other hand, persists with full, heavily spelled-out English words.

11:34.260 --> 11:41.220
You type copy Y, you type D-E-L or erase, ren for rename, D-I-R for directory, type to read a file.

11:41.420 --> 11:42.780
It's not just a different syntax.

11:43.060 --> 11:45.720
It feels like two completely different operational languages.

11:46.080 --> 11:47.480
It is an operational dialect.

11:48.140 --> 11:52.160
And this wasn't just a stylistic quirk or a preference for better grammar.

11:52.680 --> 11:57.160
This divergence created immense physical commercial pressure on the market.

11:57.320 --> 11:57.800
How so?

11:58.260 --> 12:00.500
Think about a competitor to Microsoft at the time.

12:00.500 --> 12:03.880
Let's look at DaggerDOS, which was made by Digital Research.

12:04.260 --> 12:10.140
And ironically, they were the makers of the original CPM that Microsoft had essentially cloned in the first place.

12:10.280 --> 12:10.420
Right.

12:10.700 --> 12:14.640
Digital research comes back in the late 80s, trying to reclaim the throne from Microsoft.

12:15.040 --> 12:18.660
And they built a technically superior operating system in many ways.

12:19.300 --> 12:26.800
But by 1988, when DaggerDOS launched, the install base of MS-DOS users was already so astronautically huge

12:26.800 --> 12:31.620
that DaggerDOS had to copy Microsoft's vocabulary exactly to remain commercially viable.

12:31.840 --> 12:32.260
Oh, wow.

12:32.260 --> 12:33.980
Think about it from the user's perspective.

12:34.260 --> 12:40.800
If an entire company's automated batch files or a secretary's daily muscle memory relied on typing DIR to see their files,

12:41.240 --> 12:45.180
a competing operating system couldn't suddenly ask them to learn Unix and typos.

12:45.820 --> 12:46.940
The users would revolt.

12:47.560 --> 12:48.840
The friction would be too high.

12:48.840 --> 12:53.720
So Microsoft didn't even have to write a monopolistic rule saying, you cannot compete with us.

12:53.920 --> 12:56.100
They outsourced the enforcement to us.

12:56.260 --> 12:56.620
Exactly.

12:56.920 --> 13:04.080
The users, having been trained on Microsoft's specific sprawling vocabulary, disciplined the competitors on Microsoft's behalf.

13:04.080 --> 13:13.540
If your new shiny operating system doesn't speak my highly specific dialect, I'm not buying it, no matter how good it is under the hood.

13:13.620 --> 13:15.660
You became the enforcer of their monopoly.

13:15.860 --> 13:16.000
Yeah.

13:16.000 --> 13:18.920
And this extended deeply into the documentation itself.

13:19.040 --> 13:21.840
Remember the word switch we talked about for the forward slash?

13:21.920 --> 13:22.180
Yeah.

13:22.180 --> 13:30.160
By MS-DOS 5.0 in 1991, the word switch wasn't just a quirky inherited term from a 1970s manual.

13:30.520 --> 13:35.540
It had become the foundational structuring principle for the entire built-in MS-DOS help system.

13:35.720 --> 13:36.100
Really?

13:36.500 --> 13:36.680
Yeah.

13:37.020 --> 13:41.100
The physical manuals shipped in the boxes, the error messages on the screen, the on-screen help.

13:41.180 --> 13:44.520
They all heavily, repeatedly relied on the conceptual word switch.

13:44.940 --> 13:49.260
They completely ignored the more universally accepted Unix terms like flag or option.

13:49.420 --> 13:51.140
Okay, but I have to play devil's advocate here.

13:51.340 --> 13:55.960
Because I've worked in corporate environments, couldn't this just be sheer institutional laziness?

13:56.220 --> 13:59.060
You know the mantra, if it ain't broke, don't fix it.

13:59.060 --> 14:03.600
If all your old manuals say switch, you just copy-paste that into the new manual.

14:03.600 --> 14:13.240
Is there really concrete proof in Flyxion's research that Microsoft kept it this way on purpose to trap people in a proprietary ecosystem?

14:13.740 --> 14:17.000
That is the beauty and the nuance of Flyxion's central argument.

14:17.760 --> 14:27.260
The book explicitly refuses the idea of a secret strategy meeting where executives twirl their mustaches and explicitly plotted to trap the users with the word switch.

14:27.500 --> 14:28.500
No supervillains.

14:28.640 --> 14:28.820
Right.

14:29.060 --> 14:32.860
The mechanism of control is much more mundane, but just as effective.

14:32.860 --> 14:36.000
The moat is created by the absence of reconciliation.

14:36.860 --> 14:41.780
Sustaining the divergence, just leaving the word switch in the manual, demanded absolutely nothing from Microsoft.

14:42.040 --> 14:42.460
It was free.

14:42.800 --> 14:44.380
Yeah, doing nothing is always free.

14:44.520 --> 14:45.180
But fixing it.

14:45.460 --> 14:48.420
Reconciling with the rest of the computing world by changing switch to flag.

14:48.620 --> 14:49.800
That would cost real money.

14:50.000 --> 14:51.260
You'd have to rewrite documentation.

14:51.740 --> 14:53.820
You'd confuse millions of existing users.

14:54.260 --> 14:55.960
Your support hotlines would light up.

14:56.120 --> 14:56.560
Exactly.

14:56.560 --> 15:04.740
Reconciling carried immediate, heavy internal costs for Microsoft, with absolutely zero immediate benefit to their bottom line.

15:05.300 --> 15:06.720
The cost of fixing it was high.

15:07.060 --> 15:09.020
The cost of ignoring it was zero.

15:09.280 --> 15:17.260
But the hidden cost of not fixing it is eventually passed directly onto the user, in the form of a massive barrier if they ever want to leave Microsoft's ecosystem.

15:17.260 --> 15:23.700
And Flyxion points out that we see this trap set quite deliberately in what the book calls the greenfield designs.

15:23.840 --> 15:24.340
What are those?

15:24.660 --> 15:29.780
These are moments where Microsoft had a totally blank slate, with no historical baggage holding them back.

15:29.980 --> 15:31.340
Look at 1983 again.

15:31.660 --> 15:37.780
The creation of the system configuration files config, auto-SYS, and auto-XEC.bat.

15:37.780 --> 15:38.960
Oh, man.

15:39.100 --> 15:42.340
Anyone who gamed in the 90s knows those files intimately.

15:42.600 --> 15:42.740
Right.

15:43.060 --> 15:50.400
Those are the text files that literally tell the computer how to wake up, what drivers to load into memory, and what basic rules to follow before giving you a prompt.

15:50.500 --> 15:50.780
Yes.

15:51.140 --> 15:57.760
And unlike the backslash or the word switch, there was absolutely no CPM legacy forcing Microsoft's hand here.

15:58.360 --> 16:00.140
CPM didn't use config.ys.

16:00.460 --> 16:02.960
This was fresh, unconquered territory.

16:02.960 --> 16:15.860
Now, Microsoft's engineers, the very same engineers who knew Unix well enough to borrow its hierarchical directory structure just a few months prior, also intimately knew Unix shell scripting.

16:16.080 --> 16:16.200
Right.

16:16.420 --> 16:22.100
In the Unix world, if you write a script and you want to use a variable, you denote it with a dollar sign.

16:22.860 --> 16:23.920
USCR, for example.

16:24.320 --> 16:27.240
It was an established, elegant, standard practice.

16:27.380 --> 16:32.520
But Microsoft didn't use the dollar sign in Yoto-XEC.bat, did they?

16:32.520 --> 16:33.320
No, they did not.

16:33.420 --> 16:35.900
They chose the percent sign, percent pain, antithate.

16:36.140 --> 16:36.400
Why?

16:36.500 --> 16:40.080
For reasons that no surviving internal document adequately explains.

16:40.640 --> 16:46.100
They also created entirely new proprietary directives like device for the config.ys file.

16:46.440 --> 16:47.720
They had a completely clean slate.

16:48.000 --> 16:49.600
They knew the existing industry standard.

16:49.740 --> 16:51.320
There was no legacy software to break.

16:51.520 --> 16:54.300
And they built a parallel proprietary vocabulary anyway.

16:54.420 --> 16:58.860
Because they intuitively understood that once that new vocabulary is out there, it solidifies.

16:58.860 --> 17:11.460
Once that percent sign is printed in the official manual, once it's taught in a high school typing class, once it's hard-coded into an IT department's massive library of support scripts, it is no longer just a software choice.

17:11.560 --> 17:12.680
It becomes infrastructure.

17:13.040 --> 17:14.020
It becomes concrete.

17:14.020 --> 17:20.460
And that infrastructure became incredibly hostile and incredibly complex at an astonishing speed.

17:20.680 --> 17:28.500
Which brings us to perhaps one of the most infamous, traumatizing eras of early computing, the memory management wars of the late 80s and early 90s.

17:28.500 --> 17:28.940
Oh boy.

17:29.540 --> 17:32.320
The dreaded 640 kilobyte barrier.

17:33.000 --> 17:39.740
If you were alive and using a PC in this era, just hearing the number 640K probably made a muscle in your jaw twitch.

17:39.740 --> 17:43.360
It was the defining existential struggle of personal computing for nearly a decade.

17:43.520 --> 17:50.480
The original 1981 IBM PC had a physical memory map that reserved the address space above 640 kilobytes for hardware functions.

17:50.620 --> 17:53.160
Things like video buffers, ROM chips, and adapter cards.

17:53.340 --> 17:58.160
That 640K limit wasn't necessarily a malicious design choice by Microsoft.

17:58.800 --> 18:03.400
It was a rigid physical constraint of the IBM architecture that DOS had to live inside.

18:03.400 --> 18:11.240
But as software grew, especially massive spreadsheets and complex games, 640K simply wasn't enough room to breathe.

18:11.700 --> 18:13.440
So the physical board is hard capped.

18:13.560 --> 18:14.940
The operating system is trapped.

18:15.240 --> 18:16.600
How did DOS actually handle this?

18:16.660 --> 18:23.100
Because my memory of this era is just a proliferation of acronyms that sounded like absolute technical gibberish to a normal person.

18:23.220 --> 18:26.380
It was a wildly dense proprietary grammar of workarounds.

18:27.160 --> 18:31.000
To bypass the physical limit, the industry invented different types of memory.

18:31.000 --> 18:34.120
First, you had expanded memory, or EMS.

18:34.300 --> 18:35.060
Wait, pause here.

18:35.440 --> 18:36.340
We need to explain it.

18:36.380 --> 18:37.380
Like, I'm 5 on this.

18:37.560 --> 18:39.740
What is expanded memory actually doing physically?

18:39.900 --> 18:42.000
How do you get more memory if the board won't let you?

18:42.200 --> 18:49.720
Imagine you live in a tiny apartment with one very small window, that's your 640K, but you own a massive library of books.

18:50.400 --> 18:52.620
EMS used a hardware trick called bank switching.

18:52.880 --> 18:53.660
Bank switching, okay.

18:53.660 --> 19:04.780
It took a piece of hardware on an expansion card, and it would rapidly swap different pages of memory in and out of a small reserved window inside that 640K space.

19:05.200 --> 19:09.220
You could only look at a small chunk of your massive library at a time through the window.

19:09.560 --> 19:14.020
But the hardware swapped the chunks out so fast, the computer thought it had access to all of it.

19:14.100 --> 19:14.960
Okay, that makes sense.

19:15.160 --> 19:17.780
A clever, if clunky, workaround.

19:18.040 --> 19:18.240
Yeah.

19:18.380 --> 19:20.160
But EMS wasn't the only one, right?

19:20.160 --> 19:20.820
Far from it.

19:21.280 --> 19:27.080
As processors advanced to the 80286, you suddenly had physical memory available above one megabyte.

19:27.700 --> 19:35.140
But old DOS programs couldn't natively reach it, so they created extended memory, or XMS, to manage that physical space.

19:35.380 --> 19:43.680
So now a user has to understand the difference between expanded memory and extended memory, which sound almost identical but function completely differently under the hood.

19:43.680 --> 19:51.860
And to manage them, DOS required the user to master a hyper-specific, utterly unforgiving syntax in that config.doc.ys file.

19:52.560 --> 20:01.960
By the late 80s, an average user trying to run a complex program might have to open a text editor and type device.dossymems.js just to load the extended memory manager.

20:02.100 --> 20:02.960
Just to get it to start.

20:02.960 --> 20:03.420
Yep.

20:04.040 --> 20:10.400
Then on the next line, emm386.exe no emmas to handle the expanded memory emulation.

20:10.900 --> 20:18.440
And then you had to type the absolute crown jewel of compressed hostile jargon, DOS-HI-E-H-U-M.

20:18.460 --> 20:19.260
Let's stop right there.

20:19.380 --> 20:20.660
DOS-HI-H-U-M.

20:20.760 --> 20:22.000
It's only 11 characters long.

20:22.080 --> 20:24.620
But what is it actually commanding the physical machine to do?

20:24.620 --> 20:33.600
In those 11 keystrokes, it is commanding the system to physically relocate the core operating system kernel itself into the high memory area that is the high-deed part.

20:33.960 --> 20:44.620
The high memory area was a tiny 64-kilobyte sliver of space that was only reachable because of a bizarre literal hardware bug in the Intel 8086 processor called the A20 line wraparound.

20:44.780 --> 20:48.540
Wait, they built an entire software directive around a physical bug in the silicon.

20:48.740 --> 20:49.980
Yes, and it gets worse.

20:50.060 --> 20:50.820
Of course it does.

20:50.820 --> 21:05.000
The UMB part of that command instructed DOS to map unused scattered fragments of the reserved video memory space into upper memory blocks so that you could stuff your mouse driver or your CD-ROM driver in there instead of them eating up your precious baseline 640K.

21:05.240 --> 21:06.340
That is just wild.

21:06.600 --> 21:18.420
It's essentially a magical incantation based on exploiting a hardware flaw compressed into 11 characters that gives a new user absolutely zero contextual clues about what it means.

21:18.420 --> 21:20.800
It is aggressively hostile to beginners.

21:20.820 --> 21:25.000
What's fascinating here is how Microsoft responded to this hostility.

21:25.640 --> 21:28.840
Their solution wasn't to redesign the system to remove the complexity.

21:29.420 --> 21:34.640
In 1993, with DOS 6.0, they introduced a built-in tool called MemMaker.

21:34.840 --> 21:35.940
I remember MemMaker.

21:36.020 --> 21:36.820
It felt like magic.

21:36.940 --> 21:44.280
You'd run it, the screen would flash all these colors, it would reboot your computer like three or four times, and suddenly a game that wouldn't run before magically worked.

21:44.280 --> 21:49.320
Right, but what MemMaker actually did was simply automate the writing of this complex grammar for you.

21:49.860 --> 21:58.720
It tested thousands of memory permutations in the background, and then it wrote the device high and load age directives into your config-AS file automatically.

21:58.940 --> 21:59.740
Oh, I see.

21:59.920 --> 22:02.480
But it didn't abstract the hardware conflict away.

22:02.480 --> 22:04.920
It didn't fix the underlying architectural mess.

22:05.040 --> 22:07.840
It just managed the existing jargon on your behalf.

22:07.840 --> 22:11.740
It reminds me exactly of modern tax preparation software in the United States.

22:11.800 --> 22:12.740
Oh, that's a good comparison.

22:12.740 --> 22:16.480
The underlying tax code is ridiculously, needlessly complex.

22:17.060 --> 22:27.780
But instead of the tech companies advocating for a simpler, flat tax code that anyone could understand, companies like Intuit build software that navigates the horrific complexity for you.

22:28.160 --> 22:32.920
And they profit heavily off the fact that the underlying system is an unapproachable nightmare.

22:33.280 --> 22:34.440
That is a perfect analogy.

22:34.720 --> 22:39.780
And Flixine asks the crucial question, what did this complexity actually buy Microsoft?

22:39.780 --> 22:44.440
Wait, was there a strategic benefit to making the system hard to use?

22:44.520 --> 22:46.680
That seems counterintuitive to selling software.

22:47.080 --> 22:47.420
Absolutely.

22:47.600 --> 22:50.980
There was a benefit if you look at it through the lens of switching costs.

22:51.560 --> 23:02.560
Think about the entire generation of IT professionals, corporate consultants, and teenage power users who spent hundreds of hours mastering high-mem-SYS and the dark arts of driver load orders.

23:02.680 --> 23:03.300
The experts.

23:03.640 --> 23:03.800
Right.

23:03.880 --> 23:06.800
They possessed highly specialized, incredibly lucrative knowledge.

23:06.800 --> 23:12.460
But here is the trap, that knowledge had zero transfer value outside of the MS-DOS platform.

23:12.520 --> 23:12.980
Oh, wow.

23:13.100 --> 23:23.540
If you spent three years of your life learning the granular intricacies of upper memory blocks and A20 handlers, you could not take that knowledge over to a Unix workstation.

23:23.940 --> 23:25.380
It was utterly useless there.

23:25.920 --> 23:28.360
The complexity itself was the moat.

23:28.900 --> 23:29.900
That's insidious.

23:29.900 --> 23:35.620
And we know for a fact that it didn't have to be this complex because market alternatives proved it.

23:36.440 --> 23:50.120
Competitors like Quartadex QEMM or Dakar DOS offered memory managers on the exact same physical IBM hardware that were much simpler, much more elegant, and highly automated years before Microsoft finally released MemMaker.

23:50.120 --> 23:53.600
So Microsoft's complexity wasn't strictly dictated by the hardware.

23:53.820 --> 23:55.020
It was a choice of exposure.

23:55.440 --> 24:07.040
By forcing users to interact with this hostile architecture, they effectively trained a massive global workforce of IT professionals whose primary resume-defining skill was fixing Microsoft's specific problems.

24:07.220 --> 24:07.300
Yep.

24:07.680 --> 24:13.520
If a massive corporation in 1992 wanted to switch their entire infrastructure to Unix, they wouldn't just have to buy new computers.

24:13.660 --> 24:19.060
They'd have to throw away all that hard-earned, highly specific institutional knowledge their IT staff had spent a decade acquiring.

24:19.060 --> 24:20.020
Precisely.

24:20.520 --> 24:22.480
They weaponized the switching cost.

24:23.140 --> 24:29.020
The IT staff themselves would fight management to stay on DOS, simply to protect the value of their own expertise.

24:29.760 --> 24:38.200
But once that deeply technical moat was dug, once the corporate IT world was locked in, Microsoft began shifting its vocabulary again.

24:38.300 --> 24:39.300
Where did they go next?

24:39.440 --> 24:46.380
As the 90s progressed, they moved away from technical jargon and started deploying something much more potent, consumer marketing.

24:46.380 --> 24:54.320
And this brings us to a really subtle, brilliant Observation Flyxion's work about recovery, verification, and the vocabulary of care.

24:54.740 --> 24:58.940
Up until now, we've been talking about terse commands, hardware quirks, and aggressive syntax.

24:59.460 --> 25:03.720
But in the early 90s, the language of DOS starts to get almost emotional.

25:03.900 --> 25:06.500
It reaches for a completely different psychological register.

25:06.960 --> 25:09.120
Let's look at a basic concept, file deletion.

25:09.520 --> 25:11.840
In Unix, the command to remove a file is R.

25:12.140 --> 25:14.040
It is famously terse, and it is permanent.

25:14.120 --> 25:14.320
Right.

25:14.320 --> 25:14.760
Boom.

25:14.960 --> 25:15.200
Gone.

25:15.640 --> 25:19.680
If you delete a file in early Unix, and you don't have a tape backup, it is gone.

25:20.000 --> 25:20.780
It is destroyed.

25:21.920 --> 25:24.160
DOS followed this unforgiving logic initially.

25:24.640 --> 25:30.240
But starting with DOS 5.0, in 1991, they introduced a utility called Undelete.

25:30.600 --> 25:35.820
And by DOS 6.0, they introduced a background monitoring utility called Delete Sentry.

25:35.960 --> 25:37.000
Delete Sentry!

25:37.180 --> 25:38.180
I love that!

25:38.340 --> 25:42.200
It sounds like a loyal armored bodyguard standing outside the door of your spreadsheets.

25:42.400 --> 25:43.480
It practically was.

25:43.480 --> 25:48.260
It worked by intercepting your deletion commands and secretly moving the files to a hidden cache

25:48.260 --> 25:51.020
directory on the hard drive instead of actually erasing them.

25:51.660 --> 25:54.220
But what Flyxion highlights isn't the technical mechanism.

25:54.620 --> 25:59.580
It's how the Microsoft MS-DOS Concise User's Guide described this feature to the public.

25:59.720 --> 26:00.260
What did it say?

26:00.260 --> 26:04.700
The official manual stated that files protected by Delete Sentry appear in the Undelete screen

26:04.700 --> 26:05.980
in perfect condition.

26:06.440 --> 26:08.300
And the word perfect is capitalized.

26:08.400 --> 26:09.080
Perfect condition.

26:09.300 --> 26:13.640
That sounds like how an antique dealer describes a mint in box action figure or a rare coin.

26:13.980 --> 26:14.380
Exactly.

26:14.380 --> 26:20.700
A Unix system administrator would never use evaluative subjective grading for a restored text file.

26:21.340 --> 26:26.300
To a Unix engineer, a file is merely a discrete collection of binary bytes.

26:26.720 --> 26:30.820
It is either restored identically to its previous state, or it is corrupted.

26:30.940 --> 26:31.660
It's just binary.

26:31.660 --> 26:32.840
It's math.

26:33.340 --> 26:36.340
But Microsoft deliberately chose not to use technical description.

26:37.240 --> 26:39.800
They reached for the vocabulary of physical retail goods.

26:40.360 --> 26:44.260
They invoked the concept of resale value and pristine preservation.

26:44.260 --> 26:47.120
And we see this shift in disk checking, too.

26:47.460 --> 26:52.080
Unix has a utility called SAC, which stands for File System Consistency Check.

26:52.460 --> 26:56.080
It's a blunt technical acronym, and it's been called that for four decades.

26:56.680 --> 27:01.640
Early DOS had a similar utility called CHKDSK, which was compressed and technical.

27:02.120 --> 27:06.740
But in late 1993, Microsoft replaced it with a new tool called ScanDisk.

27:06.900 --> 27:07.980
Notice what they did.

27:08.180 --> 27:11.700
They completely reversed the historical trend of abbreviating commands.

27:11.700 --> 27:17.300
They spelled it out fully, turning an administrative chore into a consumer-friendly, reassuring product name.

27:17.820 --> 27:22.660
The DOS 6 manual explicitly says ScanDisk can repair your hard disk if errors are detected.

27:22.800 --> 27:23.280
It's comforting.

27:23.360 --> 27:28.200
It is written to soothe an anxious consumer, not to instruct a systems programmer.

27:28.920 --> 27:34.380
And this comforting consumer language is heavily reinforced by a new built-in help system

27:34.380 --> 27:37.580
that cross-references itself in a completely closed loop.

27:37.740 --> 27:39.020
What do you mean by a closed loop?

27:39.020 --> 27:43.060
If you needed help, the manual told you to type help ScanDisk at the prompt.

27:43.880 --> 27:48.840
The screen would load a beautiful blue background interface that only referenced other Microsoft concepts.

27:49.260 --> 27:52.220
It created a completely self-contained world of documentation.

27:52.820 --> 27:59.060
It offered zero points of contact, zero conceptual bridges to the wider world of computing or Unix man pages.

27:59.060 --> 28:00.780
So what does this all mean?

28:01.200 --> 28:05.180
Why this sudden massive shift in the early 90s to retail marketing speak?

28:05.820 --> 28:10.680
If they already had a monopoly, if they had already won the memory management wars and locked in the IT guys,

28:10.880 --> 28:13.080
who were they trying to woo with Delete Sentry?

28:13.340 --> 28:15.520
The timing of this shift is everything.

28:16.140 --> 28:21.260
These features, Delete Sentry and ScanDisk, arrive roughly 12 to 13 years into the lifespan of DOS.

28:21.800 --> 28:25.740
There was absolutely no CPM legacy forcing them to use these consumer-friendly names.

28:26.300 --> 28:27.900
These were entirely greenfield decisions.

28:27.900 --> 28:37.900
What Flyxion argues is that this proves that by 1993, Microsoft's documentation culture had fully internally naturalized its own idiom.

28:38.900 --> 28:44.480
Unix was no longer even a reference point, not even a distant competitor to be philosophically avoided.

28:44.620 --> 28:45.340
They moved on.

28:45.500 --> 28:47.560
PC computing was exploding into family homes.

28:48.180 --> 28:49.880
DOS was no longer speaking to programmers.

28:50.120 --> 28:55.400
It was speaking directly to frightened consumers, establishing brand trust through a vocabulary of care.

28:55.400 --> 29:00.640
It's like they spent the 1980s aggressively building the concrete walls of the fortress,

29:00.920 --> 29:09.020
and in the 1990s, they started putting up wallpaper and hanging nice paintings on the interior to make you forget you were inside a fortress at all.

29:09.500 --> 29:10.340
That's exactly it.

29:10.420 --> 29:13.040
But wait, I have to push back on this idea of total isolation.

29:13.520 --> 29:15.880
Didn't DOS actually share quite a few words with Unix?

29:15.920 --> 29:18.440
It wasn't 100% proprietary divergence, right?

29:18.440 --> 29:20.660
I'm pretty sure they used some of the same terms.

29:21.000 --> 29:22.240
You're absolutely right to point that out.

29:22.340 --> 29:29.620
And Flyxion actually dedicates an entire fascinating chapter to this counterargument called The Illusion of Shared Language.

29:29.820 --> 29:30.780
Oh, I like that title.

29:30.780 --> 29:37.900
It explores what happens when a naming convention travels across platforms, but the underlying mechanical reality doesn't.

29:38.360 --> 29:43.180
DOS did share significant vocabulary with Unix, specifically around file organization.

29:43.960 --> 29:46.020
DOS uses the exact term root directory.

29:46.520 --> 29:47.800
It uses the term path.

29:47.960 --> 29:51.220
It uses wildcards, specifically the asterisk and the question mark.

29:51.220 --> 30:01.740
Okay, so if a Unix veteran from Bell Labs sat down at an MS-DOS machine in 1990 and they looked at the manual and saw the word wildcard, they would think, great, I know exactly how this works.

30:01.800 --> 30:02.720
We speak the same language.

30:02.820 --> 30:05.400
But that shared language is exactly the illusion.

30:05.740 --> 30:06.520
It is a trap.

30:06.640 --> 30:07.100
How so?

30:07.620 --> 30:10.280
To understand why, let's look at the file name boundary.

30:10.720 --> 30:14.300
In Unix, a period in a file name is just a normal character.

30:14.620 --> 30:17.620
You can name a file report dot final dot draft dot txt.

30:18.100 --> 30:19.460
You can have multiple periods, no periods.

30:19.460 --> 30:20.620
It doesn't matter to the system.

30:20.620 --> 30:24.720
But in DOS, you have the notoriously rigid 8.3 boundary.

30:25.080 --> 30:25.880
Ah, right.

30:26.080 --> 30:28.500
Eight characters, a dot, and three characters.

30:29.040 --> 30:29.540
Exactly.

30:30.040 --> 30:35.340
Exactly eight characters for the base name, a mandatory dot, and exactly three characters for the extension.

30:35.760 --> 30:38.620
And in DOS, that extension isn't just a naming suggestion.

30:39.060 --> 30:41.060
It is structurally enforced metadata.

30:41.440 --> 30:43.500
Explain structurally enforced metadata.

30:43.700 --> 30:45.260
How does the computer treat it differently?

30:45.260 --> 30:55.100
In DOS, the command parser itself literally reads the dot B-A-A or dot E-X-E or dot com extension to determine if a file is executable.

30:55.640 --> 30:58.060
The extension commands the operating system's behavior.

30:58.700 --> 31:02.920
In Unix, an extension like dot txt is just a polite hint for the human reading it.

31:03.340 --> 31:06.900
The system relies on internal file permissions to know if something is executable.

31:06.900 --> 31:11.980
So the literal period character on the keyboard does radically different mechanical work in both systems.

31:12.220 --> 31:12.780
Radically different.

31:13.240 --> 31:17.600
And this invisible divergence completely explodes when you look at how wildcards operate.

31:18.360 --> 31:28.320
In Unix, when you tap an asterisk in a command, the command line interface itself expands that wildcard before it hands the list of files over to the specific program you're trying to run.

31:28.320 --> 31:30.200
So the Unix shell does the heavy lifting.

31:30.740 --> 31:40.320
If I say delete all text files, the shell quietly gathers up all the text files in its arms and hands that explicit list over to the delete program on a silver platter.

31:40.520 --> 31:40.920
Exactly.

31:41.500 --> 31:43.440
The program never even sees the asterisk.

31:44.280 --> 31:51.540
But in DOS, wildcard matching descends directly from a dusty relic of CPM called the file control block or FCB.

31:51.780 --> 31:52.820
The SCB game.

31:52.820 --> 31:57.860
The FCB is a rigid positional template of exactly 11 characters in memory.

31:58.320 --> 32:02.320
When you tap an asterisk in a DOS command, the shell doesn't search for anything.

32:03.000 --> 32:05.600
The asterisk is merely a dumb shorthand.

32:05.840 --> 32:12.240
It just tells the system to fill the remaining empty slots in that 11-character 8.3 memory field with question marks.

32:12.560 --> 32:15.320
I compare this to the concept of linguistic false friends.

32:15.780 --> 32:20.580
You know, when you're an English speaker learning Spanish and you see the word embarazada, you think, oh, that sounds like embarrassed.

32:20.680 --> 32:21.240
I know what that means.

32:21.320 --> 32:23.580
But in Spanish, it actually means pregnant.

32:23.860 --> 32:24.160
Right.

32:24.440 --> 32:25.220
Completely different meaning.

32:25.220 --> 32:28.360
Using it wrong creates a massive, embarrassing disaster.

32:29.060 --> 32:36.300
Just because two operating systems use the English word wildcard in their manuals doesn't mean the machinery spinning underneath is doing the same thing.

32:36.660 --> 32:41.120
This must have created massive, destructive confusion for anyone trying to switch platforms.

32:41.120 --> 32:47.380
The classic, often disastrous example of this false friend is the command star dot star.

32:47.960 --> 32:59.400
In DOS, because of that rigid FCB template, typing star dot star tells the system to pad the 8-character base name with question marks and the 3-character extension with question marks.

32:59.400 --> 33:05.700
It effectively selects literally every single file in the directory, regardless of whether it has an extension or not.

33:05.860 --> 33:06.740
It's the nuclear option.

33:06.880 --> 33:07.000
Right.

33:07.320 --> 33:17.520
But in Unix, because a period is just a literal character, typing star dot star specifically instructs the system to look only for files that possess a literal period somewhere in their name.

33:17.520 --> 33:22.540
If a Unix file doesn't have an extension, which many didn't, star dot star completely ignores it.

33:22.740 --> 33:34.700
So a user migrating from Unix to DOS thinks they are doing a specific filtered search, and instead they accidentally delete their entire hard drive because the underlying mechanism betrayed the shared vocabulary.

33:34.700 --> 33:39.000
And this convergence actually sharpens Flyxion's main argument beautifully.

33:39.560 --> 33:44.560
Where DOS lacked a legacy constraint like hierarchical directories, it borrowed cleanly from Unix.

33:44.960 --> 33:53.180
But where it was bound by a legacy constraint like CPM's file control blocks for file names, the word traveled, but the mechanism didn't.

33:53.500 --> 33:55.320
The interface dictated the reality.

33:55.500 --> 33:58.460
This brings us to the grand synthesis of Flyxion's book.

33:58.460 --> 34:04.340
We spent a long time looking at the micro level, the backslashes, the convoluted memory acronyms, the false friends.

34:04.740 --> 34:08.300
Now we need to zoom out to the macro level of corporate strategy.

34:08.580 --> 34:09.720
Yes, the big picture.

34:09.920 --> 34:13.800
Because we have to address the two extreme narratives that usually dominate tech history.

34:14.060 --> 34:14.220
Right.

34:14.300 --> 34:22.760
The first extreme narrative is that this was all just a series of independent, blundering, random accidents by overworked programmers, and Microsoft just got lucky.

34:22.760 --> 34:35.840
The second extreme is the conspiracy theory that Bill Gates and Microsoft executives sat in a dark room in 1981 and hatched a brilliant evil master plan to weaponize vocabulary to trap humanity.

34:36.600 --> 34:39.560
Flyxion vehemently refuses both of these extremes.

34:40.480 --> 34:46.320
So if it's not total accident and it's not an evil master plan, what is the actual mechanism that built this prison?

34:46.320 --> 34:54.060
It is the quiet, relentless accumulation of switching costs accomplished without anyone ever making a grand switching decision.

34:54.180 --> 34:54.300
Okay.

34:54.420 --> 34:55.160
Unpack that for us.

34:55.280 --> 34:59.100
Think about an entry-level technical writer updating the MS-DOS manual in 1993.

34:59.720 --> 35:00.980
They're looking at the word switch.

35:01.260 --> 35:02.220
They're likely very smart.

35:02.320 --> 35:04.180
They probably know Unix uses the word flag.

35:04.520 --> 35:05.780
But they have the deadline.

35:06.360 --> 35:14.000
The cost of reconciling that vocabulary, rewriting the manual, fighting middle management for approval, dealing with angry, confused users who hate change,

35:14.000 --> 35:17.780
falls entirely on that individual writer and their immediate developer team.

35:17.780 --> 35:22.700
But the benefit of changing it, the benefit of making the industry standard more cohesive,

35:23.120 --> 35:30.620
or making it easier for a user to eventually switch to Unix, falls entirely on the user, or worse, on Microsoft's competitors.

35:31.000 --> 35:31.480
Exactly.

35:31.900 --> 35:33.600
The incentives are completely misaligned.

35:34.100 --> 35:40.380
So independent actors inside Microsoft, acting entirely rationally to protect their own time and budgets,

35:40.820 --> 35:43.440
naturally chose divergence time and time again.

35:44.180 --> 35:47.680
Sustaining the proprietary difference required zero action and zero budget.

35:48.260 --> 35:51.860
Fixing it required immense effort with absolutely no internal payoff.

35:52.540 --> 35:55.320
I am continually struck by the elegance of this trap.

35:55.420 --> 35:58.720
You're saying Microsoft didn't have to build the physical walls of the prison.

35:58.940 --> 36:00.820
They just handed the users the bricks.

36:01.020 --> 36:03.080
And we eagerly built it around ourselves.

36:03.280 --> 36:04.320
That's a great way to phrase it.

36:04.320 --> 36:10.860
We spent years training our IT staff, writing massive internal company guides, learning how to manipulate memory managers.

36:10.860 --> 36:16.240
By 1993, a corporate customer had a massive financial disincentive to switch to Unix,

36:16.420 --> 36:21.900
and that disincentive was paid for entirely by the customer's own sunk cost in institutional knowledge.

36:22.060 --> 36:24.680
That is the core thesis of the entire monograph.

36:24.680 --> 36:28.240
As Flyxion writes in a particularly profound passage,

36:28.240 --> 36:31.360
a moat need not be dug on purpose to hold water.

36:32.000 --> 36:37.120
The accumulated interface dependence functioned perfectly as a ruthless anti-competitive weapon,

36:37.540 --> 36:41.220
regardless of whether a single executive ever intended it to operate that way.

36:41.220 --> 36:43.380
Here's where it gets really interesting, though.

36:43.720 --> 36:47.720
Because Microsoft's history obviously doesn't stop in 1993 with DOS 6,

36:47.980 --> 36:53.040
Flyxion widens the lens significantly in the later chapters to look at the longer arc,

36:53.480 --> 36:57.940
tracing this specific corporate behavior from the days of the Altair computer in the 70s

36:57.940 --> 37:01.220
all the way up to their acquisition of GitHub in the modern era.

37:01.500 --> 37:04.580
Does this theory of the accidental moat hold up over the decades?

37:04.840 --> 37:05.940
It absolutely does.

37:05.940 --> 37:11.740
And it reveals a much broader, deeply ingrained institutional logic about how Microsoft views shared resources.

37:12.080 --> 37:14.120
Go back before DOS to 1976.

37:14.720 --> 37:17.620
A very young Bill Gates writes the famous open letter to hobbyists.

37:17.700 --> 37:18.640
Oh, I've heard of this.

37:18.640 --> 37:22.860
At the time, early computer hobbyists were freely sharing and copying

37:22.860 --> 37:26.100
Microsoft's Altair basic code on paper tapes.

37:26.640 --> 37:28.000
It was a culture of the commons.

37:28.760 --> 37:33.100
Gates argued furiously that unpaid copying was essentially theft

37:33.100 --> 37:35.480
and would starve future software development.

37:35.480 --> 37:40.340
The fundamental stance of the company was established right there as shared open commons

37:40.340 --> 37:45.120
is a dangerous condition that must be immediately corrected by a strict licensing regime.

37:45.320 --> 37:45.600
Yes.

37:46.260 --> 37:48.000
Now, jump forward to the 1980s.

37:48.480 --> 37:52.020
People assume Microsoft built DOS because they didn't understand Unix.

37:52.560 --> 37:56.760
But Microsoft actually possessed deep, world-class Unix expertise.

37:57.200 --> 37:57.380
Right.

37:57.440 --> 37:58.380
You mentioned Xenix earlier.

37:58.640 --> 37:59.060
Exactly.

37:59.260 --> 38:03.900
They developed Xenix, which is a highly successful, widely deployed microcomputer version of Unix.

38:03.900 --> 38:08.880
But when corporate priorities shifted to developing OS2 and Windows, what did they do with Xenix?

38:09.400 --> 38:11.360
They divested it to a company called SGO.

38:11.600 --> 38:12.640
They just got rid of it.

38:12.740 --> 38:13.660
They didn't open source it.

38:13.720 --> 38:15.360
They didn't merge its vocabulary with DOS.

38:15.440 --> 38:16.280
They sold it off.

38:16.680 --> 38:19.980
They kept their proprietary product lines violently distinct.

38:20.360 --> 38:23.420
Which proves the DOS divergence wasn't just born of ignorance.

38:24.000 --> 38:25.840
They knew Unix inside and out.

38:25.980 --> 38:28.580
And they actively chose to keep DOS incompatible.

38:28.580 --> 38:35.340
Then we hit the 1990s, the era of the massive browser wars, the open, universally accessible

38:35.340 --> 38:38.440
worldwide web versus Microsoft's Internet Explorer.

38:38.940 --> 38:40.860
And here the strategy becomes explicit.

38:41.380 --> 38:46.160
The internal Microsoft strategy, which was infamously revealed during the antitrust trials,

38:46.420 --> 38:49.620
was summarized by the phrase embrace, extend, and innovate.

38:49.620 --> 38:56.240
And verbally, in at least one executive meeting, it was characterized as embrace, extend, and extinguish.

38:56.300 --> 38:57.280
Extinguish. Wow.

38:57.480 --> 39:01.920
They took open web standards like HTML, embraced them by supporting them in Internet Explorer,

39:02.180 --> 39:06.660
and then critically extended them by adding proprietary capabilities that only worked in their browser.

39:06.800 --> 39:10.620
So if a web developer wanted their site to look good for the vast majority of users,

39:11.020 --> 39:15.800
they were forced to target Internet Explorer's specific, non-standard HTML tags,

39:15.800 --> 39:20.020
effectively breaking the open web for anyone using Netscape or Opera.

39:20.520 --> 39:23.000
It's the exact same logic as the DOS vocabulary.

39:23.240 --> 39:24.020
Trap them in the dialect.

39:24.020 --> 39:26.500
It is the ultimate weaponization of the interface.

39:27.100 --> 39:32.520
But then, look at the modern era, the 2014 to 2018 period under the leadership of Satya Nadella.

39:32.960 --> 39:36.100
A massive, seemingly inexplicable shift occurs.

39:36.260 --> 39:36.980
What happened then?

39:37.320 --> 39:40.040
Microsoft introduces Windows subsystem for Linux,

39:40.340 --> 39:43.400
which allows a real Linux kernel to run natively inside Windows.

39:43.400 --> 39:46.380
They spend billions of dollars to buy GitHub,

39:46.620 --> 39:49.180
which is the absolute center of open-source software development.

39:49.700 --> 39:55.620
And in 2018, they actually open-sourced the original code for MSDAOS 1.25 and 2.0

39:55.620 --> 39:58.400
under the highly permissive MIT license.

39:58.960 --> 40:01.140
Is there a predictable lifecycle happening here?

40:01.260 --> 40:03.160
It feels wildly inconsistent.

40:03.420 --> 40:06.460
It feels like they enclose the garden and build the moat when they are threatened,

40:06.580 --> 40:09.320
and they suddenly open the gates and embrace open-source

40:09.320 --> 40:13.180
when they've either totally won or completely lost a specific market space.

40:13.180 --> 40:16.680
This raises an important question about the fundamental nature of corporate behavior.

40:17.420 --> 40:20.900
Flyxion argues that the overarching rule here isn't an ideological,

40:21.500 --> 40:23.840
moral commitment to open-versus-closed software.

40:24.000 --> 40:27.980
It's entirely about controlling the specific interface where users meet the platform.

40:28.440 --> 40:33.440
Microsoft releases code into the commons only when its competitive importance has totally expired,

40:33.900 --> 40:35.480
like MSDAOS in 2018.

40:35.940 --> 40:37.880
It's a museum piece, not a threat.

40:37.880 --> 40:42.480
Or they aggressively incorporate an open standard like Linux and WSL

40:42.480 --> 40:48.080
when that compatibility directly increases the overall value and stickiness of the Windows ecosystem.

40:49.180 --> 40:52.860
When the moat no longer serves a strategic purpose to hold users in,

40:53.120 --> 40:55.860
they simply drain the water and claim to be building bridges.

40:55.860 --> 40:56.300
Wow.

40:56.920 --> 40:59.940
Okay, we have covered the command line, the history, the macro strategy,

41:00.140 --> 41:02.440
but we have to talk about what this actually felt like

41:02.440 --> 41:05.540
for the millions of regular people who bought a computer in the 1990s.

41:05.720 --> 41:08.700
The people who bought a PC to do homework or play games

41:08.700 --> 41:13.180
and who never, ever wanted to type a backslash or a memory directive in their lives,

41:13.500 --> 41:16.420
we have to talk about the divergence between marketing and reality.

41:16.420 --> 41:19.300
This is where the distance between the slick marketing surface

41:19.300 --> 41:21.740
and the brutal technical reality becomes staggering.

41:22.220 --> 41:23.400
Let's look at the graphical mask.

41:23.820 --> 41:28.840
Windows 3.1 brought graphical computing, the mouse, the windows, the icons to the absolute masses.

41:29.400 --> 41:33.240
But architecturally under the hood, it wasn't a new operating system at all.

41:33.440 --> 41:34.220
Not even close.

41:34.380 --> 41:38.880
You literally had to boot up MSDAOS, stare at a black screen with a blinking cursor,

41:39.180 --> 41:42.260
and manually type win just to launch Windows.

41:42.500 --> 41:42.940
Exactly.

41:42.940 --> 41:48.360
Windows 3.1 was, technically speaking, just a very heavy, very pretty DOS application.

41:48.960 --> 41:53.780
It occupied that exact same fragile 640k memory space we talked about earlier.

41:54.180 --> 41:57.220
And when you opened the file manager in Windows to look at your documents,

41:57.560 --> 42:01.240
you were looking at the exact same rigid 8.3 file paths,

42:01.580 --> 42:06.540
the same C drive letters, just represented as cute, friendly little yellow folder icons.

42:06.700 --> 42:08.000
The illusion of progress.

42:08.240 --> 42:12.140
The user was still fundamentally bound by DOS's ancient naming vocabulary.

42:12.140 --> 42:16.960
The interface just slapped a graphical, reassuring mask over the rigid underlying rules.

42:17.300 --> 42:19.280
But surely Windows 95 fixed that, right?

42:19.460 --> 42:21.260
Windows 95 was a cultural phenomenon.

42:21.620 --> 42:25.020
I remember the Rolling Stones playing start me up in the massive TV commercials.

42:25.540 --> 42:28.300
It was marketed to the world as a total break from the DOS past,

42:28.600 --> 42:31.540
a real, modern 32-bit operating system.

42:31.880 --> 42:32.560
It was a hybrid.

42:33.040 --> 42:37.200
And architecturally, it was arguably a brilliant, deceptive sleight of hand.

42:37.200 --> 42:41.300
The shiny core of Windows 95 did run in 32-bit mode.

42:41.880 --> 42:47.800
But the boot process that actually got the computer turned on secretly ran MS-DOS 7.0 first.

42:48.480 --> 42:53.780
The new file system, called VFF, finally allowed for those beautiful long file names we use today,

42:54.100 --> 42:54.880
complete with spaces.

42:55.080 --> 42:55.340
Right.

42:55.460 --> 42:55.960
Finally.

42:55.960 --> 43:01.700
But underneath, to maintain compatibility, it was secretly generating hidden, truncated short names,

43:02.080 --> 43:05.180
like Progra-tilde-1 for the program files folder,

43:05.500 --> 43:08.920
so that the ancient CPM-descended file control block matching

43:08.920 --> 43:11.760
would still quietly work for legacy software in the background.

43:12.000 --> 43:13.760
The DOS interior never actually went away.

43:13.860 --> 43:15.740
It was just buried deeper beneath the floorboards.

43:15.900 --> 43:20.920
Which brings us to the retail experience and the incredible irony of the visual seal of trust.

43:21.200 --> 43:25.180
Microsoft launched the highly visible Design for Windows 95 logo program.

43:25.180 --> 43:28.280
If a software box at the store had that flag logo on it,

43:28.560 --> 43:31.880
it meant the program supposedly met Microsoft's strict modern requirements.

43:32.320 --> 43:33.440
It used 32-bit code.

43:33.660 --> 43:34.880
It supported long file names.

43:35.200 --> 43:38.300
It used the modern registry instead of the old messy configuration files.

43:38.560 --> 43:44.400
For a consumer browsing a shelf at CompUSA or Best Buy, that logo was a promise.

43:44.720 --> 43:46.940
It meant someone at Microsoft checked this.

43:47.160 --> 43:47.940
It is modern.

43:48.120 --> 43:49.460
You can trust it to just work.

43:49.460 --> 43:53.780
But the visceral reality of installing hardware and software back then

43:53.780 --> 43:56.180
was completely at odds with that shiny promise.

43:56.560 --> 44:02.220
Which leads to one of the most universally traumatizing experiences of 90s computing.

44:02.480 --> 44:03.500
The blaster variable.

44:03.660 --> 44:04.660
The blaster variable.

44:04.660 --> 44:09.080
The visceral, bloody reality of configuring multimedia hardware.

44:09.800 --> 44:13.480
To get a simple sound blaster card to actually play audio in a video game,

44:14.040 --> 44:17.320
a user had to open their ancient AutoXe.bat file

44:17.320 --> 44:21.420
and blindly type an unglossed, terrifying string of hardware parameters.

44:22.020 --> 44:23.000
It looked something like this.

44:23.040 --> 44:26.080
Set blaster equals A220-I5-D1-T6.

44:26.140 --> 44:28.080
I swear I just got a cold sweat hearing those numbers.

44:28.160 --> 44:30.460
What did they even mean to a 12-year-old trying to play Doom?

44:30.640 --> 44:31.940
They meant absolutely nothing.

44:31.940 --> 44:36.400
Each letter prefix corresponded to a highly sensitive physical resource on the motherboard.

44:36.820 --> 44:38.280
A was the baseboard address.

44:38.500 --> 44:39.760
I was the interrupt request line.

44:39.920 --> 44:41.720
D was the direct memory access channel.

44:41.840 --> 44:42.980
It's just alphabet soup.

44:43.420 --> 44:48.620
An interrupt request literally meant the sound card was tapping the CPU on the shoulder to get its attention.

44:49.360 --> 44:50.940
And if you guessed the numbers wrong,

44:51.300 --> 44:55.020
or if your sound card tried to tap the same shoulder as your mouse or your printer,

44:55.760 --> 44:57.880
the game froze or the computer crashed.

44:58.200 --> 45:00.840
You often had to physically open the metal computer case,

45:00.840 --> 45:02.620
pull out the physical sound card,

45:02.940 --> 45:06.720
and manually move a tiny plastic jumper across tiny metal pins

45:06.720 --> 45:10.500
just to change the IRQ to match what you typed in the text string.

45:10.720 --> 45:13.680
I compared this experience to going to a high-end restaurant

45:13.680 --> 45:16.360
with a shiny Michelin star plaque on the front door.

45:16.900 --> 45:20.320
You walk in expecting luxury, expecting a seamless experience,

45:20.580 --> 45:22.660
and the Maithra Day hands you a wrench

45:22.660 --> 45:25.160
and tells you that you have to assemble the kitchen stove yourself

45:25.160 --> 45:29.280
using cryptic, ancient runes before they'll even consider cooking your meal.

45:29.280 --> 45:34.540
The immense distance between the reassuring, designed-for-Windows-95 seal on the box

45:34.540 --> 45:39.380
and the nightmare-of-the-blaster variable perfectly encapsulates Flyxion's overarching theme.

45:39.800 --> 45:44.120
It is the vast gulf between the confidence the system's graphical surface offered to consumers

45:44.120 --> 45:50.780
and the highly specialized, proprietary, hostile fluency its interior actually required to function.

45:50.780 --> 45:53.620
It is wild how intimate this relationship was.

45:53.700 --> 45:55.200
It wasn't just abstract code.

45:55.300 --> 45:56.440
It was physical frustration.

45:56.980 --> 46:00.940
Which brings us to the final fascinating section of Flyxion's book,

46:01.100 --> 46:03.220
Zoom, Tutorial, Homophone.

46:03.680 --> 46:04.780
These are the sensory epilogues.

46:05.680 --> 46:10.820
It has about how this operating system actually rewired our brains and our bodies on a physical level.

46:11.040 --> 46:12.820
Let's start with how it rewired the brain.

46:12.820 --> 46:17.280
Microsoft Paintbrush, which was bundled for free with Windows 3.0,

46:17.620 --> 46:21.740
it had a very specific zoom function fixed at an 8x magnification.

46:21.880 --> 46:22.540
Now, it wasn't Photoshop.

46:22.660 --> 46:24.400
It was a very basic, clunky tool.

46:24.860 --> 46:27.480
But when a child used that zoom tool for the very first time,

46:28.000 --> 46:30.900
it was a profound paradigm shift in human understanding.

46:31.100 --> 46:33.440
Because they zoomed in on a smooth curve they just drew

46:33.440 --> 46:35.820
and realized it wasn't a solid pencil line at all.

46:35.920 --> 46:36.740
It was jagged.

46:36.840 --> 46:39.100
It was made of little, discreet, editable blocks.

46:39.400 --> 46:39.820
Pixels.

46:39.820 --> 46:40.580
Exactly.

46:41.440 --> 46:43.340
Drawing on physical paper is continuous.

46:44.140 --> 46:47.180
Graphite wears into the fibers in a smooth gradient.

46:47.780 --> 46:52.420
But Paintbrush visually revealed that digital images are discrete mathematical structures.

46:52.940 --> 46:55.380
You could click on a single isolated pixel

46:55.380 --> 47:00.300
and change its color to neon green without affecting the overall shape of the line.

47:00.920 --> 47:04.480
It fundamentally, permanently changed how an entire generation

47:04.480 --> 47:07.300
understood the composition of a visual image.

47:07.300 --> 47:10.140
And it wasn't just our visual brains that were rewired.

47:10.260 --> 47:11.420
It was our physical hands.

47:11.820 --> 47:15.080
I vividly remember the Windows 3.1 interactive tutorial.

47:15.080 --> 47:19.060
It was essentially a formal, mandatory curriculum for the human hand.

47:19.580 --> 47:21.820
Before you could effectively use the operating system,

47:22.000 --> 47:25.680
a mouse lesson explicitly taught you the new physical grammar of computing.

47:26.100 --> 47:28.960
It made you practice, step by step, until you got it right,

47:29.140 --> 47:32.680
move the mouse, left click once, double click rapidly, click and hold to drag.

47:33.340 --> 47:36.920
Then it moved to Windows Basics, how to maximize a window, minimize it, restore it,

47:36.980 --> 47:38.200
grab the corner to resize it.

47:38.460 --> 47:42.340
And you couldn't move on to the next screen until the software confirmed

47:42.340 --> 47:44.560
you executed the physical movement correctly.

47:45.560 --> 47:50.720
Just like the typed vocabulary of copy Y and DIR forcefully trained a previous generation

47:50.720 --> 47:56.540
of IT workers, the Windows 3.1 tutorial explicitly trained a generation's hands

47:56.540 --> 47:58.560
in a proprietary physical grammar.

47:58.900 --> 48:01.560
Though, Flyxion notes it wasn't entirely proprietary.

48:02.560 --> 48:07.540
Interestingly, in 1992, Nintendo released the Super NES Mouse,

48:07.720 --> 48:09.800
which was bundled with the game Mario Paint.

48:09.880 --> 48:11.220
Oh, I loved that game.

48:11.220 --> 48:15.260
Millions of kids learned the exact same physical vocabulary, point, click,

48:15.740 --> 48:17.920
drag on a video game console in their living room,

48:18.200 --> 48:20.300
at the exact same time they were learning it on a PC.

48:20.800 --> 48:24.700
It shows that while Microsoft desperately wanted to control the vocabulary on the screen,

48:25.100 --> 48:27.340
the physical device itself, the invention of the mouse,

48:27.500 --> 48:29.480
constrained what the human hand could be taught.

48:30.160 --> 48:33.200
There are absolute limits to corporate enclosure when physics gets involved.

48:33.320 --> 48:36.400
Which brings us to perhaps the most hilarious, humbling limit of all.

48:37.220 --> 48:38.340
The linguistic accident.

48:38.960 --> 48:40.100
The name DOS itself.

48:40.100 --> 48:45.640
It is the perfect, ironic closing note for Flyxion's incredibly dense book.

48:46.180 --> 48:48.780
The acronym DOS stands for Disk Operating System.

48:49.300 --> 48:52.080
It descends purely from CPM and 86 duress.

48:52.520 --> 48:56.260
It has absolutely zero intended connection to any human romance language.

48:56.420 --> 49:01.180
But visually, DOS, it is identical to the Spanish word DOS, meaning the number two.

49:01.180 --> 49:04.680
A complete, staggering linguistic coincidence.

49:05.380 --> 49:09.960
But it became an unavoidable reality for hundreds of millions of Spanish speakers around the world.

49:10.580 --> 49:14.020
A massive corporation can meticulously engineer its interfaces.

49:14.260 --> 49:16.240
It can studiously avoid Unix vocabulary.

49:16.500 --> 49:19.380
It can build an impenetrable moat of switching costs

49:19.380 --> 49:23.220
and completely accidentally name their flagship monopoly product

49:23.220 --> 49:26.060
the number two in one of the most spoken languages on earth.

49:26.060 --> 49:29.860
It is the ultimate proof that no matter how big the moat is, you cannot control everything.

49:30.100 --> 49:32.360
And that really is the journey of this deep dive.

49:32.740 --> 49:38.360
From a hastily inherited CPM slash to deliberate documentation loops keeping the word switch alive

49:38.360 --> 49:42.140
to the absurd 640k memory management wars of the 90s.

49:42.280 --> 49:45.780
All of these seemingly minor, uncoordinated, budget-driven decisions

49:45.780 --> 49:50.400
coalescing into a massive, invisible fortress of switching costs that define the modern world.

49:50.400 --> 49:53.420
We didn't even realize we were laying the bricks for our own enclosure.

49:54.580 --> 49:57.840
As we wrap up this deep dive, I want to leave you, the listener,

49:57.980 --> 50:00.500
with a final thought to mull over as you go about your day.

50:01.060 --> 50:04.400
Today, we are surrounded by entirely new walled gardens.

50:04.920 --> 50:08.920
Whether it's the highly specific, incredibly dense prompt engineering you have

50:08.920 --> 50:11.500
to learn to get good results out of different AI models,

50:11.500 --> 50:17.860
or the proprietary, heavily locked-down sharing ecosystems of Apple versus Android.

50:18.560 --> 50:23.320
Ask yourself, what accidental backslashes are we currently accepting right now, today,

50:23.420 --> 50:27.660
that will silently become the inescapable, invisible moats of the 2030s?

50:27.980 --> 50:30.160
That is exactly the right question to ask.

50:30.520 --> 50:34.240
The interfaces we take for granted today, the buttons we blindly click,

50:34.300 --> 50:36.800
are already righting the rigid boundaries of our future.

50:37.160 --> 50:39.240
Thank you so much for taking this deep dive with us.

50:39.240 --> 50:42.220
Keep questioning the interface, keep looking for the invisible walls,

50:42.380 --> 50:43.500
and we will see you next time.

